# Permissions and ownership

Linux protects files and directories using a permission system that controls who can access a file and what they can do with it. Every file and directory has an owner, a group, and a set of permissions. This access control model is one of the main reasons Linux is considered a secure operating system.

## Ownership

Every file has:

Owner (User) – The user who owns the file.
Group – A group of users who may share access.
Others – Everyone else on the system.

## File Permissions

Linux uses three basic permissions.

Read (r)

Allows viewing the contents of a file.

For directories, it allows listing the contents.

Write (w)

Allows modifying a file.

For directories, it allows creating or deleting files inside.

Execute (x)

Allows a file to be run as a program or script.

For directories, it allows entering (accessing) the directory.